WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:06.970
AI is driving power requirements dramatically.

2
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.830
Led by Meta, the OCP Rack and Power Projects community

3
00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:15.130
developed with HPR to meet this demand.

4
00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:22.230
HPR features a 6 -inch deeper and 65 -kilogram heavier

5
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:27.910
rack, primarily to increase busbar capacity from 18 kilowatts

6
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:32.690
to 92 -kilowatts minimum, and upgraded power shelves from 3

7
00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:38.310
-kilowatts to 5 .5 -kilowatts PSUs, totaling 33 -kilowatts per

8
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:43.890
shelf. HPR also improved grounding and added room for an

9
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.230
extra power shelf, allowing for 3 power and 3 BBU

10
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:53.870
shelves. AC whips transitioned from 20A

11
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.790
to 30A in North America.

12
00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:00.410
Today's most recent plans for HPR go up to 8

13
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:05.370
power shelves. The HPR rack's mechanical structure is simple.

14
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.990
We bolted a welded extension to the standard ORv3 frame

15
00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.170
using existing mounting points.

16
00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:17.590
This extension supports the deeper busbar, providing protection and

17
00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:22.350
grounding. All standard accessories like security doors and rear heat

18
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.370
exchanges can be used.

19
00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.510
The extension also supports up to 6 AC cable whips

20
00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.470
with integrated cable management.

21
00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:36.250
To maintain structural integrity and accommodate the added weight,

22
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.870
HPR adds ballast masses at the front of the frame

23
00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.410
to ensure stability and pass UL qualifications.

24
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.670
When designing the busbar, we considered going wider, but to

25
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:55.610
accommodate platform concerns, we went deeper instead, adding 125mm

26
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:59.130
for a total depth of 190mm.

27
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.480
This increased the busbar weight from 15kg to 80kg, necessitating

28
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.160
more robust mounting features.

29
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.820
Instead of two screws, we now use eight at the

30
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.820
bottom and a bracket at the top, for four mounting

31
00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:21.440
points. Through simulations and thermal testing in 2024, the

32
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.740
first iteration of HPR achieved 155kW from the rack, even

33
00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:31.140
though real -world applications might yield slightly less.

34
00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.020
To support the higher power, we upgraded to 30A AC

35
00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:41.260
whips for North America, while EU maintains the 32A IEC

36
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.800
309 standard with two plugs instead of one.

37
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:51.620
Moving from ORV3 to ORV3 HPR also shifted the

38
00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.640
rack grounding point to the busbar side, connecting the 50V

39
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.540
return and busbar cage with metal blocks.

40
00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.400
This allows us to remove IT gear input connector ground

41
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.220
contacts. We also changed the busbar cage material to low

42
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.980
-conductivity steels to increase contact resistance and mitigate potential

43
00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:18.620
ground current. The HPR power shelf, still 1U, now

44
00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.000
outputs 33kW with 5 .5kW PSUs.

45
00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:29.240
We upgraded busbar output connectors to 700A and designed dedicated

46
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.200
thermistors to monitor busbar clip temperature.

47
00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:38.880
HPR PSUs significantly increased peak power capability, supporting

48
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.060
136 % rated power for 50ms and 160 %

49
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.460
for 400 microseconds, crucial for GPU workloads.

50
00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:55.240
Finally, Bennett, together with its OCP community partners, developed

51
00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:59.960
an Active Power Monitoring Module, PMM, with an MCU for

52
00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.880
automatic address allocation and critical parameter consolidation.

53
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.020
All our PSU, BBU, PMM, and AC Whips specs are

54
00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:14.840
available on the OCP Rack and Power Wiki, with the

55
00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.880
overall rack spec coming soon.

